  description="China-to-Oman shipping cost and transit time: WHFI v1 rate benchmarks, the ×1.1025 duty + VAT stack shared with the UAE, Sohar vs Muscat gateway logic, and the FCL/LCL crossover band."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Shipping cost & transit time China to Oman' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Shipping cost &amp; transit time from China to Oman: rates, Sohar vs Muscat, and the ×1.1025 stack</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        For years the honest answer on Oman freight was “no published rate — request a quote”. That
        changed this month: WHFI v1 added the first China→Oman benchmark rows, all LOW confidence.
        This page puts those numbers next to the part that decides your invoice either way — the
        ×1.1025 statutory stack, the Sohar-vs-Muscat gateway question, and the FCL/LCL crossover,
        which on Oman’s thin lane arrives earlier than most importers expect.
      </p>

      <section class="section container">
        <blockquote class="tldr">
          <p>
            <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Oman clears at ×1.1025 (5% duty + 5% VAT) — the same multiplier
            as the UAE, so tax decides nothing between those two. What decides is the lane: WHFI v1
            benchmarks 20ft FCL at $1,750&ndash;$2,500, LCL at $110&ndash;$190/CBM and air at
            $2.40&ndash;$3.50/kg (all LOW), while the LCL floor sits roughly 3.7× above the UAE’s
            published base bracket. And Muscat has no container seaport any more — Port Sultan
            Qaboos moved to cruise duty in 2014 and cargo shifted to Sohar, so “delivery to Muscat”
            is a Sohar discharge plus a truck leg.
          </p>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified rules from indicative planning notes. The 5% duty and 5%
        VAT lines are verified; every freight number on this page is a LOW-confidence aggregate —
        treat it as a floor, and verify with your carrier before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Statutory stack</span>
          <span class="fact-value">CIF × 1.1025 <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Typical 19 days <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Container gateway</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Sohar (OMSOH) <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">LCL benchmark</span>
          <span class="fact-value">$110–$190/CBM <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Anatomy of a China-to-Oman quote: freight, the statutory stack, and destination fees</h2>
    <p>
      A “shipping cost from China to Oman” quote is really three cost groups stacked on one another.
      <strong>Group one</strong> is the international move: ocean freight for FCL or LCL, or air
      freight billed on chargeable weight. <strong>Group two</strong> is the statutory stack — 5%
      customs duty on the CIF value, then 5% VAT on the duty-inclusive base, a compound worth
      ×1.1025. <strong>Group three</strong> is the destination fee stack: terminal handling,
      documentation, customs brokerage, Bayan processing, inland transport and, if free time is
      missed, demurrage or detention.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most comparisons stop at group one, and on most lanes that is merely incomplete. On Oman it
      can flip a decision. The country is one of the few GCC markets where the port question is
      genuinely open — Sohar in the north, Salalah in the south, and a capital whose old port no
      longer takes commercial box ships at all. A Muscat-bound consignment routed to the wrong
      coast carries a materially different inland bill, and no ocean rate table will show it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page keeps the three groups separate on purpose. Every table below states its basis: the
      freight benchmarks are port-to-port planning ranges; the statutory layer is arithmetic you
      can verify; the destination layer stays “request the schedule” until a terminal or broker
      gives us a number. If a quote mixes the layers without saying so, that is the first thing to
      challenge.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. WHFI v1 rate card: what China→Oman freight benchmarks at</h2>
      <p>
