  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Sohar vs Muscat: where containerised cargo actually lands</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the fact that reorganises every Oman logistics plan: <strong>Muscat has no commercial
      container seaport</strong>. Port Sultan Qaboos — Mina Qaboos, the country’s traditional
      gateway in Muttrah — ceased commercial operations in 2014 and was transformed into a cruise
      ship port; commercial cargo moved to Sohar, with Muscat road congestion cited as one reason
      for the shift. The port services summaries are blunt about it: from 2014 the port caters
      mainly to cruise liners and commercial activity sits in Sohar.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the real gateway map is a three-way choice, not a two-port split:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sohar ({soharUnlocode})</strong> — the northern container gateway and the default discharge
        point for Muscat, Al Batinah and the northern interior. It sits outside the Strait of
        Hormuz on the Gulf of Oman, which is why it keeps appearing in crisis-routing plans.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Salalah ({salalahUnlocode})</strong> — the deep-water southern hub on the Arabian Sea, best for
        Dhofar demand and for transshipment relays rather than for a Muscat delivery.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Muscat airport (MCT)</strong> — the air option for urgent, light or high-value
        cargo into the capital region.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The practical decision logic is straightforward once you know the map. Cargo destined for
      Muscat city, Sohar’s industrial belt or anywhere in northern Oman lands at Sohar and trucks
      inland. Cargo for the southern governorates lands at Salalah — routing it through Sohar means
      crossing the country by road. And a quote that names “Muscat port” for a container should
      trigger a question, because what it usually means is a Sohar discharge plus a truck leg that
      someone may or may not have priced. The full port comparison lives in the
      <a href="/middleeast/oman-ports/">Oman ports guide</a>; for the cost side, the rule of thumb
      is to pin the discharge port on the bill of lading before rates are locked.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. Inside Sohar Port &amp; Free Zone, Oman’s industrial gateway</h2>
      <p>
        Sohar is not a generic box port with a shed next door. The SOHAR Port and Freezone complex
        is managed by the Sohar Industrial Port Company, a 50:50 joint venture between the
        Sultanate of Oman and the Port of Rotterdam, and it pairs a deep-water industrial port with
        an adjacent free zone built around refineries, smelters, metals and logistics parks. The
        container terminal (OICT) runs under a Hutchison-linked joint venture. Rotterdam DNA shows
        in the model: the port was designed around industrial clusters, not just throughput.
      </p>
      <p>
        For importers, three consequences matter more than the org chart:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Heavy and industrial cargo fits here.</strong> Project equipment, steel,
          machinery and construction inputs are Sohar’s second nature; discharge and handling
          capabilities are built for them in a way that a consumer-goods terminal is not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The free zone changes your options — pending confirmation.</strong> Free zones
          can offer storage, processing and re-export arrangements that mainland import does not.
          The specific treatment your goods qualify for is not published as a schedule we can
          verify, so confirm it with the operator or your broker rather than assuming a package.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resilience is part of the pitch.</strong> Sohar faces the Gulf of Oman outside
          the Strait of Hormuz, so vessels can call without transiting the chokepoint — a
          structural argument that has become concrete during the 2026 Hormuz disruption, when
          cargo for other Gulf markets was re-routed through Omani ports and moved overland.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The counterweight is density. Sohar is a less-congested, faster-clearing alternative to
        Jebel Ali and Jeddah — that is its verified congestion edge — but it also runs on fewer
        sailings and a thinner carrier roster, which is exactly where small-market friction
        concentrates: schedule windows, minimum charges and less negotiating tension between
        lines.
      </p>
      <SourceList sources={gatewaySources} title="Sources — Sohar & Muscat gateway facts" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. The small-market premium: Oman’s rate floors against the UAE’s</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious question for a Gulf importer is “how much more does Oman cost than the UAE?”.
      The honest answer takes two tables, because the published numbers do not sit on the same
      shelf. Here is the comparison as the data actually supports it:
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'segment', label: 'Segment' },
        { key: 'oman', label: 'Oman (source, confidence)' },
        { key: 'uae', label: 'UAE (source, confidence)' },
        { key: 'reading', label: 'What the comparison can carry' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Row confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={uaeCompareRows}
      caption="Cross-source comparison. Confidence levels differ by row — treat every reading as indicative, not as a measured premium."
    />
    <SourceList sources={[...rateSources, ...sourcesByIds(['tonlexing'])]} title="Sources — Oman vs UAE comparison" />

    <p class="mt-2">
      Two things follow from that table. First, the <strong>FCL comparison cannot settle the
      question</strong>: Oman’s rows are LOW-confidence aggregates from one source family, while
      the UAE’s headline comes from a spot index including surcharges. Publishing a “premium” from
      that pairing would be manufacturing a conclusion the data cannot carry — so we don’t. If you
      need FCL parity or premium, force two quotes onto the same basis (same box, same origin,
      same week, port-to-port) and compare those.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second, where floors <em>are</em> comparable, the small-market signal is real. Oman’s LCL
      floor of $110/CBM sits about 3.7× above the UAE’s $30 base bracket, and 5.5× above the
      corridor-wide $20 floor. Thin consolidation volume is the most plausible driver: fewer
      boxes to pool, less frequent consolidations, higher minimum charges. The same friction shows
      up in transit — the LCL window is 25–35 days against 18–28 for FCL — and in carrier density,
      where Sohar runs a thinner roster than Jebel Ali by any route-data measure.
    </p>
    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>How to price it:</strong> model Oman as “UAE-class tax + small-market friction”.
      Budget from the LOW rows, but hold the budget loosely — the platform cross-check on the same
      lane ($6,758–$7,744 all-in indicative, September 2026) shows how much the basis can move the
      number before the market does. Two dated quotes beat one benchmark.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Building the CIF value Oman Customs assesses</h2>
      <p>
        Oman duty is calculated on CIF, so the first step in any landed-cost model is assembling a
        defensible CIF value: cost of goods plus insurance plus freight. Freight is not a tax-free
        line — it is part of the base that duty, and then VAT, are charged on. The second step is
        the statutory arithmetic: 5% duty on CIF, then 5% VAT on the duty-inclusive base, which
        compounds to <code>CIF × 1.05 × 1.05 = CIF × 1.1025</code>.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 1 — Assemble the CIF value</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'layer', label: 'Cost layer' },
          { key: 'calc', label: 'How to obtain it' },
          { key: 'amount', label: 'Indicative amount' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={cifRows}
        caption="CIF 10,000 USD is an illustrative round figure only. Replace it with your actual declared value, freight and insurance."
      />
      <SourceList sources={taxSources} title="Sources — CIF and landed-cost layers" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">Step 2 — Duty, VAT, then the fees that follow</h3>
      <p>
        For a USD 10,000 CIF shipment, the 5% duty is USD 500, bringing the duty-inclusive base to
        USD 10,500. VAT at 5% applies to that base — not to the original $10,000 — so it lands at
        USD 525 and the statutory total is USD 11,025. Destination fees (THC, documentation, Bayan
        clearance, inland transport, possible demurrage or detention) sit on top and are not
        quantified in any public snapshot; request them itemised.
      </p>
      <p>
        One discipline note before the arithmetic below: under-declaring the CIF value is not a
        saving. The value must reflect cost, insurance and freight; an artificial invoice invites
        revaluation, penalties and delay, and any correction flows through both the duty and the
        VAT layer. On a 1.1025 stack, a $2,000 revaluation is $205 of duty and VAT you were going
        to pay eventually anyway — with interest, in the worst sense of the word.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. The ×1.1025 arithmetic on a $10,000 CIF — and the $25 compounding effect</h2>
    <p>
      The stack is short enough to do in your head, which is exactly why it is worth doing before
      accepting anyone’s “about 10%” hand-wave:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Duty: $10,000 × 5% = <strong>$500</strong>.</li>
      <li>Duty-inclusive base: $10,000 + $500 = <strong>$10,500</strong>.</li>
      <li>VAT: $10,500 × 5% = <strong>$525</strong>.</li>
      <li>Statutory landed cost: <strong>$11,025</strong> — and the effective multiplier is 1.1025.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The $25 question: why is VAT $525 and not the $500 a naive “5% plus 5%” reading implies?
      Because the second 5% is charged on the duty-inclusive base. The duty itself becomes taxable
      value. That is the compounding effect — small on a $10,000 consignment, but it scales
      linearly with declared value, and it is the kind of line that separates a quote that was
      actually calculated from one that was estimated.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'destination', label: 'Destination' },
        { key: 'multiplier', label: 'Statutory multiplier' },
        { key: 'working', label: 'Working' },
        { key: 'landed', label: 'Landed statutory cost' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={stackCompareRows}
      caption="Same $10,000 CIF across four GCC destinations. Qatar and Kuwait currently have no general VAT on imports."
    />
    <SourceList sources={[...taxSources, ...sourcesByIds(['qatar-customs', 'kuwait-customs'])]} title="Sources — GCC statutory comparison" />

    <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
      <Card title="The formula in one line">
        <p class="lead">
          <strong>CIF × 1.05 × 1.05 = CIF × 1.1025</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Duty first, VAT on the duty-inclusive base. Add operational fees on top of that
          statutory figure.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Why the tax is not the story">
        <p>
          Oman and the UAE land on the same ×1.1025 — a $0 difference on $10,000 CIF. Saudi Arabia
          is $1,050 heavier; Qatar and Kuwait are $525 lighter. Between Oman and the UAE, every
          cost difference you will actually experience comes from the freight lane, not the tax
          code.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>8. FCL vs LCL to Sohar: a crossover band, not a fixed 15 CBM</h2>
      <p>
        Most GCC cost pages repeat a “crossover at about 15 CBM” rule. On Oman’s published rows,
        that rule deserves an asterisk, because both of its inputs are now quantified — and both
        are LOW confidence, which widens the answer into a band rather than a point.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
          { key: 'fcl', label: 'FCL' },
          { key: 'lcl', label: 'LCL' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={fclVsLclRows}
        caption="FCL and LCL comparison on the WHFI v1 rows; all inputs LOW confidence."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — FCL vs LCL inputs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">The crossover arithmetic, shown honestly</h3>
      <p>
        The crossover is where per-CBM pricing overtakes the flat box rate: crossover CBM = FCL
        rate ÷ LCL rate. With Oman’s rows:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Cheapest box against the most expensive LCL: $1,750 ÷ $190 ≈ <strong>9.2 CBM</strong>.</li>
        <li>Most expensive box against the cheapest LCL: $2,500 ÷ $110 ≈ <strong>22.7 CBM</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So the honest statement is a <strong>9–23 CBM crossover band</strong>. Below roughly
        9 CBM, LCL’s per-unit pricing wins on any reading of the rows. Above roughly 23 CBM, the
        container is winning on any reading. In between, the answer depends on which quotes you
        actually get — and if you habitually act at “15 CBM”, you are using the midpoint of a band
        that could be wrong in either direction. Two structural notes push the practical decision
        earlier than the midpoint: LCL carries a minimum charge on small volumes, and it adds CFS
        handling and the slower 25–35 day window. Neither appears in the flat arithmetic above.
      </p>
      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Oman’s small-market twist:</strong> because consolidation volume is thinner than
        Dubai’s, a small shipment may sit longer for consolidation or carry a higher minimum
        charge. Before choosing LCL, request the CFS cutoff, the minimum CBM and the next
        consolidation sailing date. The corridor-wide crossover logic is covered in more depth in
        the <a href="/middleeast/fcl-vs-lcl/">FCL vs LCL guide</a>.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>9. Air freight to Muscat: reading the published tiers</h2>
    <p>
      WHFI v1 also added the first China→Oman air rows (via Junqing, LOW confidence): a
      $2.40–$3.50/kg band with visible weight tiers — 45–99 kg at $3.10–$3.50, 100–299 kg at
      $2.70–$3.10, and 300 kg+ at $2.40–$3.20. Door-to-door service adds roughly $1.50–$3.00/kg
      on top, which is the line most first-time air shippers forget when comparing against a DDP
      quote.
    </p>
    <p>
      Air is billed on chargeable weight — the higher of actual and volumetric weight — and the
      volumetric convention in air freight is gentler than courier: roughly 1 CBM per 167 kg
      (the ÷6000 divisor). A 2 CBM shipment therefore bills around 333 kg even if it physically
      weighs far less. At the published tiers, that is about $800–$1,070 at the 300 kg+ level
      before door-to-door adders. The number is not automatically disqualifying — it is simply
      five to six times a sea quote's ballpark, so it has to buy something: speed, stock-out
      avoidance, or security for high-value goods.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two cautions. These tiers come from a single aggregated source, so treat them as structure
      (weight bands move the rate) more than as current market levels. And express courier
      pricing behaves differently again — falling sharply at higher weights, with minimums that
      bite small parcels. If your shipment is under about 50 kg, compare courier and consolidated
      air before assuming either wins; the <a href="/middleeast/air-freight-from-china-to-oman/">Oman air freight
      guide</a> lays out the full mode decision.
    </p>
    <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — air freight" />
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>10. What else moves the rate: surcharges, season, and free-time discipline</h2>
      <p>
        Below the headline rate sits a layer of surcharges and operational levers that decides as
        much of your invoice as the base number does. The table keeps the recurring ones explicit;
        none of the amounts are published in the snapshot, so each is a request-the-schedule item
        rather than a figure to plug in.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
          { key: 'effect', label: 'Effect on cost or time' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={factorRows}
        caption="Operational context. Only 5% duty and 5% VAT are verified — unquantified fees stay LOW confidence and require a fee schedule."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — cost & transit factors" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage and detention</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is charged by the terminal when an imported container sits
        inside the port beyond the free time allowed after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is
        charged by the ocean carrier when the box stays outside the port beyond its free time
        after collection. Both clocks are per-day, both differ by line and terminal, and neither
        schedule is published in the snapshot — request both before booking. The way to stay off
        both meters is unglamorous: file the Bayan declaration before the vessel arrives, keep HS
        codes and documents ready, screen restricted goods early, and book trucking before the
        container is discharged.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Cost discipline for a two-coast corridor</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Confirm the discharge port (Sohar vs Salalah) before the bill of lading is cut — and remember “Muscat” is a truck destination, not a sea one.</li>
        <li>Consolidate smaller orders into fewer, fuller FCL moves once volume crosses the 9–23 CBM band.</li>
        <li>Compare FCL and LCL all-in quotes at the same destination point, on the same week.</li>
        <li>Classify the HS code and screen restricted goods before booking.</li>
        <li>Request the Bayan declaration before vessel arrival to protect free time.</li>
        <li>Negotiate the destination fee schedule, not just the headline freight rate.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>11. Clearance mechanics: Bayan, documents, and the Omani conformity screen</h2>
    <p>
      Oman’s baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF value</strong> under the GCC Common
      External Tariff, with <strong>5% VAT</strong> on the duty-inclusive base — the ×1.1025
      stack. Higher lines exist for specific goods (cement, some paints and polyurethane, alcohol)
      and basic foods and GCC-origin goods can qualify for duty-free treatment. The HS code is
      therefore the first cost decision, and it is made before quoting, not after.
    </p>
    <p>
      The clearance sequence runs through <strong>Bayan</strong>, Oman’s electronic declaration
      system:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Classify the goods with the correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting or booking.</li>
      <li>Confirm importer registration, permits and any restricted-commodity approval before shipment.</li>
      <li>Prepare the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or air waybill, and certificate of origin.</li>
      <li>File the <strong>Bayan</strong> declaration through Oman Customs.</li>
      <li>Pay the assessed 5% duty and 5% VAT, pass inspection if selected, and take delivery.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Two clarifications that save real money in confused quotes. First, <strong>ISF is a United
      States requirement</strong> and has nothing to do with Oman — the advance-filing step here
      is the Bayan declaration. Second, <strong>SABER and SASO are Saudi systems</strong> and do
      not apply to Oman either; Oman runs its own standards and conformity regime, under which
      certain products need certification before arrival. The specifics for a given product are
      not published in our snapshot — confirm the applicable process with Oman Customs or your
      broker ahead of shipping.
    </p>
    <SourceList sources={[...taxSources, ...omanPortSources]} title="Sources — Oman customs & conformity" />
  </section>

